e use conflict graphs to show that computing the flip distance between two non-crossing spanning trees is NP-hard, even for point sets in convex position. Interestingly, the result still holds for more constrained flip operations, concretely, compatible flips (where the removed and the added edge do not cross) and rotations (where the removed and the added edge share an endpoint). Additionally, we present new insights on the diameter of the flip graph, by this directly extending the line of research from [BKUV SODA25]. Their lower bound is based on a constant-size pair of trees, one of which is of a type we refer to as stacked. We show that if one of the trees is stacked, then the lower bound is indeed optimal up to a constant term, that is, there exists a flip sequence of length at most 14/9 ⋅ (n − 1) to any other tree. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFinite groups with isomorphic non-commuting graphs have the same nilpotency property - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/wiki/Finite_groups_with_isomorphic_non-commuting_graphs_have_the_same_nilpotency_property)\nciteturn6search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Finite groups with isomorphic non-commuting graphs have the same nilpotency property ... Authors Hamid Shahverdi ... graph isomorphism nilpotent group non-commuting graph\n# Finite groups with isomorphic non-commuting graphs have the same nilpotency property\n\nFrom MaRDI portal\n\nJump to:navigation, search\n\nDOI10.1016/J.JALGEBRA.2023.12.007MaRDI QIDQ6140037zbMATH OpenOpenAlexFDO\n\nAuthors Hamid Shahverdi\n\nPublication date 19 January 2024\n\nPublished in Journal of Algebra (Search for Journal in Brave)\n\nFull work available at URL https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jalgebra.2023.12.007\n\nzbMATH Keywords\n\ngraph isomorphismnilpotent groupnon-commuting graph\n\nMathematics Subject Classification ID\n\nGraphs and abstract algebra (groups, rings, fields, etc.) (05C25) Finite nilpotent groups, (p)-groups (20D15) Arithmetic and combinatorial problems involving abstract finite groups (20D60)\n\nCites work\n\n  * A conjecture related to the nilpotency of groups with isomorphic non-commuting graphs\n  * Commutativity pattern of finite non-Abelian p-groups determine their orders.\n  * scientific article; zbMATH DE number 3520437 (Why is no real title available?)\n  * scientific article; zbMATH DE number 857570 (Why is no real title available?)\n  * Non-commuting graph of a group.\n  * Non-commuting graphs of nilpotent groups.\n  * On the number of solutions of Goormaghtigh equation for given \\(x\\) and \\(y\\)\n  * Simple groups are characterized by their non-commuting graphs\n\nCited in\n\n(2)\n\n  * Group nilpotency from a graph point of view\n  * Neighborhoods, connectivity and diameter of the nilpotent graph of a finite group\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAsymptotic dimension for covers with controlled growth - University of Birmingham (https://research.birmingham.ac.uk/en/publications/asymptotic-dimension-for-covers-with-controlled-growth-2/)\nciteturn6search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: last week; Dyn. 6 (2012), no. 4, 639–658), proving that there is no regular map H^{2}→T_{3}×Y whenever Y is a bounded degree graph with at most polynomial growth, and no quasi-isometric embedding whenever Y has subexponential growth.\n\n# Asymptotic dimension for covers with controlled growth\n\n  * David Hume\n  * , John M. Mackay^{*}\n  * , Romain Tessera\n\n^{*}Corresponding author for this work\n\n  * Mathematics\n\nResearch output: Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\n\n## Abstract\n\nAbstract We prove various obstructions to the existence of regular maps (or coarse embeddings) between commonly studied spaces. For instance, there is no regular map (or coarse embedding) H^{n}→H^{n−1 }× Y for n⩾3n, or (T_{3})^{n}→(T_{3})^{n−1 }× Y whenever Y is a bounded degree graph with subexponential growth, where T_{3} is the 3-regular tree. We also resolve Question 5.2 (Groups Geom. Dyn. 6 (2012), no. 4, 639–658), proving that there is no regular map H^{2}→T_{3}×Y whenever Y is a bounded degree graph with at most polynomial growth, and no quasi-isometric embedding whenever Y has subexponential growth. Finally, we show that there is no regular map F^{n}→Z≀F^{n−1} where F is the free group on two generators. To prove these results, we introduce and study generalisations of asymptotic dimension that allow unbounded covers with controlled growth.\n\nOriginal language  | English\nArticle number  | e70043\nNumber of pages  | 36\nJournal  | Journal of the London Mathematical Society\nVolume  | 111\nIssue number  | 1\nEarly online date  | 17 Dec 2024\nDOIs  | \n\n  * https://doi.org/10.1112/jlms.70043\nPublication status  | Published - Jan 2025\n\n## Access to Document\n\n  * 10.1112/jlms.70043Licence: Creative Commons: Attribution (CC BY)\n\n  * HumeD2024AsymptoticFinal published version, 405 KB Licence: Creative Commons: Attribution (CC BY)\n\n  * https://londmathsoc.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1112/jlms.70043\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAsymptotic dimension for covers with controlled growth - Hume - 2025 - Journal of the London Mathematical Society - Wiley Online Library (https://londmathsoc.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1112/jlms.70043)\nciteturn6search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; Theorem B.There is no quasi-isometric embedding from $$\\mathbb {H}^2$$ to $$T_3\\times Z$$ when $$Z$$ has subexponential growth. ... For $$n\\geqslant 2$$ there is no regular map from $$\\mathbb {H}^{n+1}$$ to $$\\mathbb {H}^n \\times Z$$, whenever $$Z$$ is a bounded degree graph with subexponential growth.\n### 1.1 Non-embedding results\n\nWe will be particularly interested in the following problem.\n\nProblem 1.1.Consider spaces $$X$$ and $$Y$$ of exponential growth (typically products of symmetric spaces and regular trees) such that there is no coarse embedding/regular map from $$X$$ to $$Y$$. Prove that there is no coarse embedding/regular map from $$X$$ to $$Y\\times Z$$ where $$Z$$ is a space with subexponential growth (e.g. $$\\mathbb {R}^k$$).\n\nSpecific instances of this problem in the literature include [2, Question 5.2], which asks whether there is a regular map $$\\mathbb {H}^2 \\rightarrow T_3 \\times \\mathbb {R}$$, and [3, Question 6.2]: assume $$X$$ and $$Y$$ are direct products of non-Euclidean symmetric spaces such that there exists a coarse embedding from $$X$$ to $$Y\\times \\mathbb {R}^k$$ for some $$k$$, does this imply that $$\\operatorname{rank}(X)\\leqslant \\operatorname{rank}(Y)$$?\n\nOur first result provides a strong negative answer to Benjamini–Schramm–Timár's question.\n\nTheorem A.There is no regular map $$\\mathbb {H}^2$$ to $$T_3\\times Z$$ when $$Z$$ has polynomial growth.\n\nthen\n\n$$$$\\begin{equation*} \\operatorname{corank}(X)+\\ell \\leqslant \\operatorname{corank}(Y)+\\ell ^{\\prime }. \\end{equation*}$$$$\n\nLet us discuss a few examples. The non-existence of a regular map from $$\\mathbb {H}^{n+1}$$ to $$\\mathbb {H}^n\\times \\mathbb {R}^k$$ for any $$n \\geqslant 2, k \\geqslant 0$$ follows from previous work of the authors [24], and can most likely also be deduced as a consequence of the methods in the aforementioned result of [13]. Now we are able to replace $$\\mathbb {R}^k$$ by any graph of subexponential growth:\n\nCorollary 1.2.For $$n\\geqslant 2$$ there is no regular map from $$\\mathbb {H}^{n+1}$$ to $$\\mathbb {H}^n \\times Z$$, whenever $$Z$$ is a bounded degree graph with subexponential growth.\n\nFollowing a subtle strategy based on filling functions outlined by Gromov in [20, section 7.$$E_2$$, pp. 141–144], Bensaid proved that the rank of a direct product of non-Euclidean symmetric spaces and Euclidean buildings is monotonous under coarse embedding [3]. The monotonicity of the rank under regular maps remains open. In particular, the existence of a coarse embedding from $$T_3\\times T_3\\times T_3$$ to $$\\mathbb {H}^k\\times \\mathbb {H}^k$$ for any $$k\\geqslant 2$$ is prevented by Bensaid's monotonicity of the rank, but not the existence of a regular map. On the other hand, the existence of a coarse embedding from $$T_3\\times T_3\\times T_3$$ to $$T_3\\times T_3\\times \\mathbb {R}^k$$ for some $$k$$ is not ruled out by any of the previous results. It is now a special case of the following:\n\nCorollary 1.3.For $$n\\geqslant 2$$ there is no regular map from $$(T_3)^{n}$$ to $$(\\mathbb {H}^2)^{n-1} \\times Z$$ (in particular to $$(T_3)^{n-1} \\times Z$$), whenever $$Z$$ is a bounded degree graph with subexponential growth.\n\nNote that Theorem C does not prevent the existence of a regular map from $$T_3\\times T_3\\times T_3$$ to $$\\mathbb {H}^3\\times \\mathbb {H}^3$$, which is still open.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIndian J Pure Appl Math (2025) 56:791–812 (https://insa.nic.in/writereaddata/UpLoadedFiles/IJPAM/Vol56_2025_2_ART29.pdf)\nciteturn6search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.5 years ago; non-solvable AC-group along with some other ﬁnite non-abelian groups. ... In 2014, Abdollahi and Shahverdi [3] proved that |G1| = |G2| if G1 and G2\nare ﬁnite non-abelian nilpotent groups with irregular isomorphic non-commuting graphs. ... In 2012, Abdollahi and Shahverdi [2] veriﬁed this ... One of the objectives of this paper is to check whether (1.1) holds for\n792\nW. N. T. Fasfous, R. K. Nath\nThis conjecture was also posed by Abdollahi et al. [1] independently and veriﬁed for Sn, An, D2m, any ﬁnite\nnon-solvable AC-group along with some other ﬁnite non-abelian groups. Though this conjecture was refuted\nby Moghaddamfar [30] in the year 2006, people continued the search for different classes of ﬁnite non-abelian\ngroups satisfying this conjecture. In 2014,